{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791308458,"nonce":"1oismKlCG0+QObXexnxusNZeUZc=","title":"HIR: price small scalar helpers with a single fallback call outside loops","message":"After the pool-cursor candidate, the NUC SOR execution-thread profile has about 15.44% of samples in LLVM zb_number_i64. Its optimized body is a small scalar load/conversion helper with one checked-reader fallback call. The hot integer path loads the payload, but callers still pay a function call around those loads. Runtime call-heavy builtin bodies retain the helper call; these library bodies are optimized before installation. The SOR region also hits its 512-instruction call-bearing budget, so increasing whole-caller budgets is not the proposed fix.\n\nInvestigate the outside-loop inline rejection for small scalar adapters: a bounded body, a primitive result, at most one ordinary fallback call, no stores/allocations, and no loops. Keep normal caller budgets, recursion exclusions and the generic fallback. Check actual emitted helper HIR and benchmark before accepting any heuristic change. Prior broader call-chain look-through and constant-argument experiments regressed other kernels; see 83ce145669b5a3b04e1e61b20fa3c8c0cc87502e05e5cd3a798859cc866a30d7 and e7eba40f77853d5eca2317ab1cfe177fd1a48a3f65463e4a9ace08854f3ecf20. This hypothesis needs measurement and may be rejected.","files":null}]}